<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="A2.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Reconstruction metrics for different audio tokenizers.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The audio tokenizer determines the audio token rate and, therefore, the sequence length corresponding to
the audio stream. Since we use a fixed context length (<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\star}=3256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\star}=3256</annotation></semantics></math>) and clips with at most 30 seconds, we need
a low-rate codec that still preserves perceptual quality. Here, we compare several RVQ-based codecs: a pretrained 24 kHz DAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a pretrained Higgs Audio v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boson AI, 2025</a>)</cite>, and a DAC-style tokenizer trained on the same data as the main model. In <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ B.1 Audio Tokenizer Ablations ‣ Appendix B Tokenizer Ablations ‣ The Design Space of Tri-Modal Masked Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a>, we present the reconstruction evaluation on LibriTTS-clean using PESQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rix et al., 2001</a>)</cite> and Audiobox. Among the options, only two configurations fit our token budget for 30 s audio, which we display in bold.
Based on these results, we chose the Higgs Audio v2 tokenizer with 4 codebooks decoding as the default audio tokenizer as it provides a strong and convenient rate–distortion trade-off.
As expected, increasing the number of codebooks improves reconstruction but quickly becomes impractical under our
sequence-length constraint. Consistent with prior observations for RVQ codecs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>,
we also find that training with more codebooks and decoding with fewer can preserve perceptual quality substantially
better than training the low-rate model directly.</p>
</div>
